// =============================================================================
// 22-connector-403-by-index.kql
// Tests the per-index RBAC hypothesis: is one of the seven indexes unauthorized?
// =============================================================================
//
// RUN THE DISCOVERY QUERIES FIRST
//   00-discover-dependency-types.kql and 01-discover-search-diagnostic-shape.kql.
//   File 00 gives you the live (type, name, target) triple for section 1 below;
//   file 01 gives you the IndexName_s spellings for section 2. The literals used
//   here come from the sampled customer record and are UNVERIFIED against any
//   Microsoft documentation, so substitute whatever file 00 actually reports.
//
// PURPOSE
//   Determine whether the 403s cluster on a subset of the seven indexes. A role
//   assignment scoped to six of seven indexes looks intermittent in aggregate
//   and is perfectly deterministic per index, which is exactly the signature the
//   customer is describing.
//
// WHY THIS IS THE CHEAPEST HYPOTHESIS TO DISPROVE
//   If the 403s are spread evenly across all seven indexes, per-index RBAC is
//   dead and the investigation moves to the service-wide causes: the IP firewall
//   allow-list, the APIM quota policy, or a broken connection instance. If they
//   concentrate on one or two index names, the answer is a role assignment and
//   the fix takes minutes.
//   Research: .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md
//             Lines 142-163 (D6 hypothesis 4, per-index RBAC scoping)
//
// TWO SECTIONS, AND WHY BOTH ARE NEEDED
//   The connector span does NOT carry the index name. In the sampled record the
//   target is "shared_azureaisearch/SemanticHybridSearch", which identifies the
//   connector operation, not the index. So:
//     Section 1 groups the connector-side 403s by target and conversation to
//               establish volume, spread, and whether target ever varies.
//     Section 2 groups the Search-side 403s by IndexName_s. This is the actual
//               per-index test.
//   Reading them together also answers a second question. If section 1 shows
//   hundreds of 403s and section 2 shows none, the requests never reached Search
//   at all, which points at APIM or the connector rather than at authorization.
//   That is the same conclusion 20-apim-403-triage.kql reaches from the gateway
//   side, arrived at independently.
//
// TARGET TABLES
//   Section 1: Application Insights dependencies.
//   Section 2: AzureDiagnostics, ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH".
//
// PREREQUISITES
//   * Section 1: Copilot Studio telemetry export to Application Insights.
//   * Section 2: Search diagnostic setting sending OperationLogs.
//   Enable both with: scripts/Enable-DiagnosticSettings.ps1
//
// HEALTHY VS UNHEALTHY
//   Healthy    : zero rows in both sections.
//   Per-index  : section 2 returns 403s concentrated on a subset of index names.
//                Hypothesis confirmed. Audit the role assignments on those
//                indexes and compare them against a working one.
//   Service-wide: section 2 returns 403s spread evenly across all seven. This is
//                not RBAC scoping. Look at the IP firewall allow-list and
//                publicNetworkAccess instead.
//   Never arrived: section 1 returns rows and section 2 returns none. The
//                rejection happened before Search. Go to 20-apim-403-triage.kql.
// =============================================================================

// --- SECTION 1: connector-side 403 spread ------------------------------------
let lookback = 7d;
dependencies
| where timestamp > ago(lookback)
| where type == "Connector"                  // UNVERIFIED, from the sampled record
| where name == "Azure AI Search"            // UNVERIFIED, from the sampled record
| where resultCode == "403"
| extend
    ConversationId = tostring(customDimensions["attributes.conversationId"]),   // UNVERIFIED
    ChannelId      = tostring(customDimensions["attributes.channelId"]),        // UNVERIFIED
    DesignMode     = tostring(customDimensions["attributes.DesignMode"])        // UNVERIFIED
| summarize
    Failures              = count(),
    DistinctConversations = dcount(ConversationId),
    Channels              = make_set(ChannelId, 10),
    DesignModes           = make_set(DesignMode, 5),
    AvgDurationMs         = round(avg(duration), 1),
    FirstSeen             = min(timestamp),
    LastSeen              = max(timestamp),
    SampleOperationId     = take_any(operation_Id)
    by target
| order by Failures desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SECTION 2: Search-side 403 by index. THIS IS THE ACTUAL PER-INDEX TEST.
// Run this one and read the IndexName_s column. Compare the index list against
// the full set of seven from file 01 section 2: an index that appears here and
// nowhere in the success traffic is the smoking gun.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 7d;
// AzureDiagnostics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
// | extend StatusCode = toint(resultSignature_d)
// | summarize
//     Total       = count(),
//     Forbidden   = countif(StatusCode == 403),
//     Succeeded   = countif(StatusCode == 200),
//     FirstDenied = minif(TimeGenerated, StatusCode == 403),
//     LastDenied  = maxif(TimeGenerated, StatusCode == 403),
//     SampleQuery = take_anyif(Query_s, StatusCode == 403)
//     by IndexName_s
// | extend ForbiddenPct = round(100.0 * Forbidden / Total, 2)
// | order by Forbidden desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
